findings.body:
`generate_github_mirror_url_from_base` used to call `cdn.set_path(github_url)` to put the original GitHub URL under the mirror. That line was removed, so the function now returns the mirror's base URL unchanged and ignores `github_url` entirely:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="33-40"}

With `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR=https://mirror.example.com/`, every GitHub download (linuxdeploy, the AppImage plugin, WiX, NSIS) fetches `https://mirror.example.com/` instead of the real asset. `download_and_verify` callers then fail with a confusing `HashError`. The AppImage downloads in `appimage.rs` don't verify a hash, so they write the mirror's index page to disk as an executable. This breaks the documented feature (`ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES.md`), and the PR's stated goal doesn't call for it.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="37-39"}
    .map(|mut cdn| {
      cdn.set_path(github_url);
      cdn.to_string()
    })

The existing tests only cover the `_TEMPLATE` variant. Please add one for the base-URL variant.

This download used to go through `download(url)`, which picks its agent via `create_agent_and_url`. It now calls the global `ureq::get` directly:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/windows/util.rs" lines="62-69"}

The global agent doesn't use `RootCerts::PlatformVerifier`. So right after `webview2_guid_path` succeeds with the system-cert agent, the actual installer download fails on the same machine behind a TLS-intercepting proxy. That's the exact case this PR is supposed to fix. The change also loses the `Downloading` log line and duplicates the body-read code from `download()`.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/windows/util.rs" lines="65-68"}
    std::fs::write(&file_path, download(url)?)?;

With that change, the new `std::io::Read` import can be removed.

This condition was flipped from `crate_lock_packages.is_empty()` to `!crate_lock_packages.is_empty()`. TLS certificates have nothing to do with it:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/helpers/cargo_manifest.rs" lines="194-205"}

The old code was effectively dead: an empty list joins to an empty string, so `lock_version` was never set. The new code runs whenever the lockfile has more than one version of the crate, so `tauri info` output now shows `x.y.z (a, b)`. That may well be the intended behavior. But it's a user-visible change hidden in a feature PR, with no changelog entry and no test. Either move it to its own PR or document and test it here.

`generate_github_alternative_url` still returns `ureq::agent()` for mirror URLs:

::code{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="42-50"}

So users who set `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR[_TEMPLATE]` don't get system certificates, even though corporate and internal mirrors are the most likely place for a private CA. This path also has no proxy configuration (the code didn't set one before either). The changelog promises system certs "when downloading tools". Use `base_ureq_agent()` here as well.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="49-49"}
    .map(|alt_url| (base_ureq_agent(), alt_url))

`base_ureq_agent` repeats the whole builder chain in two `#[cfg]` blocks and ends with an explicit `return agent;`, which isn't idiomatic Rust. `crate_latest_version` in the CLI writes a third copy of the TLS setup:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="56-73"}

You can make only the TLS step conditional:

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="56-73"}
pub(crate) fn base_ureq_agent() -> ureq::Agent {
  let builder = ureq::Agent::config_builder().proxy(ureq::Proxy::try_from_env());
  #[cfg(feature = "platform-certs")]
  let builder = builder.tls_config(
    ureq::tls::TlsConfig::builder()
      .root_certs(ureq::tls::RootCerts::PlatformVerifier)
      .build(),
  );
  builder.build().into()
}

For the CLI, consider a small shared helper, or reuse a public helper from `tauri-bundler`, so the two crates can't drift apart.
